{
    "title": "Fisheries of the Exclusive Economic Zone Off Alaska; Demersal Shelf Rockfish in the Western, Central, and West Yakutat Regulatory Areas of the Gulf of Alaska",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 05, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-05/pdf/2025-22141.pdf",
    "summary": "The federal fisheries agency just made two year-end moves in Alaska: it stopped fishermen from keeping demersal shelf rockfish in parts of the Gulf of Alaska because the annual limit has been reached, and it shifted some unused Pacific cod quota in the Bering Sea and Aleutians from one group of vessels to another so the fish can still be harvested before the year ends.\n\nFor the Gulf of Alaska, the agency is prohibiting retention of demersal shelf rockfish in the Western, Central, and West Yakutat areas through December 31. This is a short, end-of-year action taken after new catch data showed the 2025 limit was met. There are narrow exceptions for certain gear types under specific monitoring rules, but for most vessels, any rockfish caught in these areas must now be released. The main upside is conservation: stopping retention helps prevent overshooting the annual limit and supports long-term stock health. The downside is economic and operational. Fishermen targeting other species who unintentionally catch these rockfish will face more discards and lost revenue, and some communities could see reduced income from this fishery for the rest of the month.\n\nIn the Bering Sea and Aleutians, the agency is reallocating a small portion of unused Pacific cod (about 200 metric tons) from catcher vessels using pot gear to catcher-processors using the same gear, effective immediately through December 31. The goal is to make sure the full annual allowance is used responsibly rather than left in the water due to timing or capacity constraints. This benefits catcher-processors and their crews, who gain access to fish they can still harvest this month, and it can support a steadier supply for buyers. Those who are giving up the unused share might view it as a lost opportunity if conditions change, and shoreside processors could miss out on some landings from the original fleet. Still, the reallocation reflects the reality that the initial group was not expected to harvest that portion this year.\n\nBoth actions are based on the most recent catch data and are limited to the final weeks of 2025.",
